protections for  religious freedom up next we learn about  another founding father and the  financial scandal that landed him in  prison  Robert Morris was an incredibly  important American founder but it's true  that not very many people know about him  he was the financier of the American  Revolution so-called because he served  as the superintendent of Finance towards  the end of the American Revolution and  he really helped put that early  government on a sound financial footing  when it was in big trouble at the end of  the war so he was a money man he's also  not very well known because of what  happened to him at the end of his life  he messed up pretty bad he was one of  the richest guys in the country after  the American Revolution he was a very  important senator from Pennsylvania but  at the end of his life he ended up  losing basically all of his fortune and  went to debtors prison and when he came  out of debtors prison the country did  not have very fond feelings for him and  so he ended up being more or less  forgotten for the next few generations I  like to tell the story of Robert Morris  is something like rags to riches and  then back to rags again because he  started off from pretty humble  circumstances he was born in Liverpool  in England we don't think that his  parents were married originally his dad  ended up coming over to America as a  tobacco factor as a as a worker in the  tobacco industry and so he was brought  over as a 13 or 14 year old boy to live  with his father in Maryland and ended up  not liking Maryland and wanted to go to  the big city and so his father arranged  for Robert Morris to move up to  Philadelphia and once he settled in  Philadelphia he found his niche  he ended up apprenticing at a merchants  firm and proved to be really a prodigy  when it came to trading and accounting  and doing all the things that merchants  were doing at the time and so he rose  quickly in the ranks and a chief partner  at a firm when he was was quite young so  he didn't have a whole lot of formal  education it was all on the job and that  was pretty much characteristic of him  throughout his life he he wasn't one of  the more intellectual or cerebral  founders he was a guy who liked doing  and he had a kind of a common touch as  well he'll I  to drink you like to eat he liked to  sing songs at the pub with his club club  friends and so he was more of a rowdy  rougher around the edges founder than  most of us think of when we think of  that set of folks he was less of a  politician in what we traditionally  think of of the term when the big  question of Independence finally came in  1776 whether to sign the Declaration of  Independence or to support that movement  and so Robert Morris was I'd say  hesitant about declaring independence he  didn't think it was something that would  be helpful in the short term he thought  maybe in a long term this would be  something to work towards and so he was  not he took himself out of the meeting  when it came time to actually vote for  independence but in the next month or so  once they brought the declaration around  for though all the signatures he signed  on and pledged pledged his fortune for  the war effort and kind of arrests  through his fate in with everybody else  as we know Philadelphia was a key city  for initiating the American Revolution  this is where the Continental Congress  was meeting and this is where a lot of  American trade was taking place and so  when the Congress started meeting there  and when the colony of Pennsylvania  started to think about what it was going  to do in terms of the crisis with Great  Britain he was swept into that as as a  rich guy as a merchant who had something  to say about how trade was going to  happen or was not going to happen with  the mother country and so he ended up  being a member of the Continental  Congress quite early he was also active  in the colony or the state politics once  the state had declared its independence  and so he was important for the  Continental Congress early on because he  was one of those traders that the  Congress tapped to help them find arms  and ammunition for the war effort the  colonies up and down the East Coast  didn't have a big army or a big Navy  ready to go and so they needed to find  those kinds of supplies to make the war  effort happen and so he was tapped as  one of those  behind-the-scenes workers that were  arranging for loans and for weapons and  for supplies to make the war effort go  there's a anecdote I don't know if it's  true or not but it's kind of  symbolically true and that when one of  the generals was looking for ammunition  and there was no ammunition to be had  one of Morris's ships came in and Morris  was able to find lead ballast in the  bottom of a ship and that they then  turned into cartridges the other kind of  anecdote that is often told about Morris  during the war that kind of personifies  his approach to keeping the war effort  going was when the soldiers were headed  towards Yorktown they paused and kind of  put in a little bit of a mutiny to say  that they did not want to go any further  without getting paid they had not been  paid for months and so Morris was able  to receive hard money from the French  that he was able to borrow and then take  down directly to the soldiers so that  they would have pay enough that they  could send back to their families so  that they could feel good about  continuing on to Yorktown and then  ultimately winning the war but mostly  how he helped with the war effort was  providing money for it finding loans  either among the American population or  abroad with France or the Netherlands or  Spain or some of the other countries  that were willing to loan this fledgling  government money so that they were able  to pay their bills we're all pretty much  familiar with what a Continental dollar  was worth and it wasn't worth very much  and so there needed to be a constant  supply of