nd so it was something  that the locals ended up calling  Morris's folly and so the house ended up  standing in for really Morris's  financial poor decisions and so the  house became a symbol for Morris's  failed ambition because it was never  really completed and it was torn down  shortly afterwards and so rather than  proceeding at a more cautious pace or  trying to wrap up some of these big  dealings he keeps on with them and so he  starts to really get in trouble I would  say in a significant way in 1795 or 1796  where he and one of his main partners a  guy named John Nicholson have to end up  basically creating their own type of  currency they called them eminence after  Morris and Nicholson's and they would  try to sign for each other little checks  that they were then try to pay their  creditors for and as they tried to do  this over a period of a year or two they  had thousands and thousands  of dollars that they had essentially  created that after a short period of  time would become due with real money or  with real credit and that ended up being  just a spiral that he and his partner  could not get out of  and so by 1797 things had gotten so bad  for Morris that he actually retreated  out of his family's home in the center  of Philadelphia and left to live on his  country estate outside of town about  three miles of the Schuylkill River and  he retreated into his home there and the  sheriff's were trying to apprehend him  and bring him to debtors prison but  there was a rule that says that the  sheriff's could not break and enter into  someone's private home and so as long as  he was behind his bar doors he could  maintain his operations and he thought  again that he could keep pulling himself  out of this that the tide would soon  turn that it was just around the corner  that these sales were gonna happen and  that his credit would come back into  full bloom again but by 1798 February he  had run out of time and he saw the  writing on the wall and he eve