 Trammell and his book the  fourth branch of government the fourth  branch of government is actually a  contested term some people refer to it  as the media but my co-author and I  would like to co-opt that and reinvent  the fourth branch to be the power of the  people and we believe that America has  grown to the point where our democracy  needs to grow some too and the fourth  branch is the people's branch it's the  people's participation in our government  and democracy  I think guy's premise for what he wanted  to put in the book came from his  experience as an ordinary citizen in our  democracy experiencing some of the  frustrations and with partisan  partisanship and the failure of the  two-party system and gridlock and  government and so on for me my  perspective changed after I ran for  Congress in 2014 and Eric Cantor's old  seat it opened my eyes to the  dysfunctionality of many things in our  democratic processes as well as our  government part of the disconnect comes  from the fact that we're in uncharted  territory we've never been a nation of  this many millions of people we've never  been a nation that has an economy that's  moved from manufacturing into services  and technical side of the economic  equation we've never had a democracy  that had to function with this much  diversity and this many complications in  the worldwide economy so we need we need  a democracy that's able to adapt to this  new world that we live in and  unfortunately it seems like we're stuck  in some of our old ways to cite just one  example we haven't made a meaningful  constitutional amendment in almost 50  years  and yet on average we've amended it  every 20 years or so and so we seem to  have lost the ability to keep changing  and that seems to me to be one of the  key problems right now  the role of social media has really  impacted our democracy and the way our  election immerse in campaigning I before  2014 when I ran for office I viewed  social media as kind of a a funny  distraction but in my campaign our  social media transformed from pictures  of me kissing babies and meeting with  people in retirement homes and instead  became an active place where the debate  took place between the opposing  candidates and where issues were fleshed  out in meaningful ways and this  transformation took place over four  months and was significant and by the  end of the campaign social media was one  of our main vehicles it was difficult  for example for me to debate with my  opponent to establish that protocol and  to get him to agree to it but we had  quite a vigorous debate on a daily basis  in social media through our campaigns  and then more recently in the Trump  presidential campaign we've seen yet  another level of innovation in the use  of social media and whether you like it  or not I think we will see that it will  have transformed the way we're going to  be doing elections from here on out my  co-author and I would like to suggest  that social media also presents an  opportunity for people who can  participate in democracy at a higher  level and we would like to see  technology updated in the way that  people participate Evo ting is something  we have a chapter devoted to but there  are many other ways technology can  become involved as well the chief  benefit in the most general sense is  that this is the convenience we live our  lives with I shop on my smartphone I  take online courses on my smartphone I  pay my credit card bills and do my  banking on my smartphone and of course I  network with people why can't I  participate in my democracy through my  smartphone now in terms of social media  I can of course have an opinion and be  involved with people but guy and I would  like to see a more formal level of all  where we actually implement Evo ting and  allow people to participate in democracy  through their smart technology there is  resistance to Evo ting for example as a  form of technological participation in  democracy the resistance is primarily  centered around security concerns but  guy and I argue that if people want to  try to sway an election in a fraudulent  way technology is not the only way for  them to do that and so the problem is  the motivation of people and not the  technology itself and we've actually  interacted with some people at the  University of Virginia for example who  believe that it's very possible to  implement a safe form of a voting that  is almost a hundred percent fraud free  it involves some technical things that  I'm not adept with but when it's  explained to me it makes perfect sense  I think the resistance also comes from  the fact that we've been in about fifty  years of gridlock where we're just not  willing to look at the Constitution  we're not willing Congress's passing  half the laws they did 50 years ago more  complicated world less work from  Congress and so we feel like we need a  breakthrough of some sorts and  technology is one way we can do that I  think technology has had both positive  and not so positive impacts on political  activism on the one hand social media  and other forms of technology have made  it much easier for people to get  involved in just a few short minutes  having an opinion contacting someone  joining a crowd online so to speak but I  think it's also discouraged people from  some of the traditional ways of being an  activist for example we're standing here  in Richmond as the General Assembly is  meeting and we have lots of people next  to us who are coming in to meet with  their delegates or their Senators and I  think nothing replaces that face-to-face  contact and so there is a downside to  the technology but this is the world we  live in and we need to make it  compatible with our democratic processes  and we need to make it easy for lots of  people to participate with the  implementation of a voting I think it's  a little bit different than just  standard issues around social media I  think Evo ting is an access issue in  the Frontier Days access mint court day  and driving 20 or 30 miles in a wagon to  get to the county seat and making sure  you've filled out a paper ballot and  stuffed it in the box our tools are  different today but the process is the  same we just want to make it easier for  people to actually vote I still feel  like there are many obstacles too many  people making it difficult for them to  vote  Annie voting would be a way for us to  make it much easier for the vast  majority of people to participate even  an off-year elections and particularly  local elections and there have been some  locales that have experimented with a  voting and the sky has not fallen and so  it may be time for us to try it at a  state level or to try to look at this a  little bit more closely  I see technology and democracy as  evolving if we go back to 2008 and when  President Obama ran his first campaign  we saw innovation with social media in  that campaign it was a grassroots  campaign it was a populist movement with  lots of wide bases across the country  across many states and social media fed  into this it actually was more than  cheerleading it actually helped grow the  movement then in 2016 we've seen another  evolution with this to another level  where we're actually counting who has  more followers we're keeping track of  who tweets what in terms of factual  content and so I think in 2018 2020 in  the presidential election we're likely  to see a further evolution of this and  the question is how can we use this  social media to get the ordinary person  more involved in our democracy and in  our elections in the case of President  Trump I think most people realize by now  that he is the one typing on his  smartphone and sending it out all days  all hours of the day and night and this  again was something new in presidential  politics as far as I know and I think  there's an upside to that I think in  terms of populism there were a lot of  people who respected him for that and  felt like they were able to connect  him personally that there weren't layers  of campaign staff that that were  filtering what he was saying and the  fact that he occasionally had a misstep  with what he said actually endeared him  to some people now the flip side of that  and I fall into this camp I that  somebody who's a candidate and  ultimately became president should be  very thoughtful about what they put out  in the public space and impulsivity and  Twitter sometimes don't mix so I think  there's an upside to it and a downside  to it I think one reason it's difficult  to for people to get involved beyond  their smartphone or beyond their social  media is that in the last few decades  we've really become programmed to live  our lives through that technology and  through social media my wife and I have  a rule that once a week on date night we  can't take our our phones with us and I  think politicians need this role too  sometimes all of us perhaps but I think  this technology has become so ubiquitous  so commonplace that we perhaps are  losing track of some of the other things  that we need to do in a democracy we  need to have face-to-face conversations  we need to have groups of people  actually be together physically see each  other react to each other in real time  be able to see body language be able to  understand what people are saying in  context and politicians do you engage in  this during campaigns they meet with  crowds they meet with people but I think  social media on the downside has a  tendency to disconnect us from that for  people who read the fourth branch of  government what I'd like for them to  take away is that our founders were wise  they gave us tools and so we should not  feel helpless now we may not all agree  on what those tools should be there may  be people who think that Evo ting is  just not practical in the book we argue  that the electoral college is out of  date but I'm sure there are people who  argue that it is very necessary and  important the point is we need to have  these conversations because we've  stopped updating our democracy and yet  the world has continued to change so the  main message in the book is that we need  to be ready to change  and it may not be this and it may not be  that but we need to have a conversation  about it because if we stay where we are  we'll fall further behind book TV is on  Twitter and Facebook and we want to hear  from you tweet us twitter.com slash book  TV or post a comment
 

